\id ACT - Aperto Bible (English) v4.7.3 (2026-06-13)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Acts
\toc1 The Acts of the Apostles
\toc2 Acts
\toc3 Ac
\mt1 Acts

\c 17
\s1 Uproar in Thessalonica
\p
\v 1 The road carried them through Amphipolis and Apollonia\add , the great Roman highway,\add* and on at last to Thessalonica. There was a synagogue there.
\v 2 Paul went in, as he always did,\f + \fr 17:2 \fk KONTEXT \fq as he always did \ft Luke chooses this phrase deliberately. In his Gospel he used almost exactly these words for Jesus, who went to the synagogue on the Sabbath "as was his custom" (Luke 4:16). Paul is not leaving his Jewish life behind — he is continuing it. The way the announcement begins is inside the synagogue, not outside it.\f* and for three Sabbaths running he worked through the Scriptures with them.\add  Not preaching at them. Arguing, opening the texts, taking their objections, answering them.\add*
\v 3 Passage by passage he laid it out: the Messiah had to go this way, through suffering, through death, and rise again. \add And every time, the same landing:\add* "This Jesus I'm telling you about: he is the Messiah."
\p
\v 4 Some of them were persuaded. They threw in with Paul and Silas, along with a great many Greeks who worshiped the God of Israel\add  without being Jews themselves\add*\f + \fr 17:4 \fk KULTUR \fq Greeks who worshiped the God of Israel \ft These were the "God-fearers" — a recognized category in the cities of the eastern Mediterranean: non-Jews who worshiped the one God of Israel and kept the ethical commandments of the Torah without formally converting. Inscriptions from Aphrodisias and Sardis name them explicitly. Many were women of social standing; with the framework of Israel's Scriptures already familiar to them, they were the readiest hearers the message could find.\f*, and a good number of the city's leading women.
\p
\v 5 But the others were seized by jealousy. \add They watched their own standing drain away, passing to a stranger: the trust, the followers, everything that had been theirs.\add* So they pulled together a few idlers from the marketplace, worked up a crowd, and threw the city into an uproar.\f + \fr 17:5 \fk HARM \fq the others \ft The Greek says "the Jews" stirred up the trouble. It means specific members of the Thessalonian synagogue — not the Jewish people. Paul is himself a Jew; so is Silas; the dispute runs inside Judaism, among Jews, over a messianic claim. For centuries Christian interpretation turned sentences like this into an accusation against "the Jews" as such — a reading that is false to the text, and that fed persecution and worse. This passage is the record of one local conflict, nothing more.\f* They stormed Jason's house, expecting to find Paul and Silas inside and drag them out before the assembly.
\v 6 They weren't there. So the mob seized Jason and a few of the believers instead, and dragged them before the politarchs\add , the city leaders,\add*\f + \fr 17:6 \fk KULTUR \fq politarchs \ft "Politarch" was not common Greek — for a long time scholars treated the title as Luke's mistake, since it turned up nowhere else in ancient literature. Then archaeology produced more than thirty inscriptions across Macedonia naming exactly this office, nineteen of them from Thessalonica alone. It was the locally precise word for the city's elected board of magistrates, with the power to demand a bond from citizens and to pass cases up the Roman chain of command. Where other ancient writers grew vague, Luke knew the city.\f* shouting as they came: "The men who've turned the empire upside down are here too,
\v 7 and Jason has taken them in. Every one of them defies Caesar's decrees. They say there is another king: Jesus."\f + \fr 17:7 \fk KONTEXT \fq another king — Jesus \ft Under Roman law, proclaiming a rival ruler was treason — the gravest charge that could be brought. It is the exact accusation laid against Jesus before Pilate: that he claimed to be a king (Luke 23:2). What is striking is that the believers do not deny it. The narrator lets the charge stand, as though it were accidentally true: to call Jesus "king" is to re-order your loyalties — and every power built on loyalty can feel it.\f*

\p
\v 8 \add It was the most dangerous thing anyone could say in that city.\add* The crowd shook. The magistrates shook with them.
\v 9 They made Jason and the others post bond\add , a pledge against any further trouble,\add* and then, finally, let them go.
\s1 Beroea: A People Who Examined Day by Day
\p
\v 10 That same night the believers sent Paul and Silas on to Beroea\add , off the main road\add*. And there, too, the first thing they did was go to the synagogue.
\p
\v 11 These were people of a different kind, more open than the ones in Thessalonica had been. They took the message in eagerly, and they didn't stop there. Day after day they went back to the Scriptures and examined them,\f + \fr 17:11 \fk KULTUR \fq examined \ft The Greek word for this examining comes from the language of the courts: it means cross-examination, the testing of a claim against the evidence. The people of Beroea were not pious nodders — day after day they took up the Scriptures and held Paul's claims against them. And they did it together, in the synagogue, not each one alone. Where this verse was later made the banner of a "let everyone judge for themselves" individualism, what was lost was exactly what makes it remarkable: a shared, patient, argumentative reading, in which eagerness and rigor are not opposites.\f*\f + \fr 17:11 \fk LEBEN \fq day after day \ft What Beroea models is a way of reading that refuses the choice between enthusiasm and scrutiny: the people praised here are the ones who checked. You can start there, with nothing required of you but honesty: read a passage and ask, plainly, whether it holds. For those further along, the practice deepens when it becomes daily and shared — a fixed time, the same texts, and other people free to disagree with you, because a reading tested only against yourself is barely tested at all. And it reaches past the page. The Berean habit — claims held against the evidence, in company, over time — is also how a workplace stays honest, how a friendship survives a hard conversation, how a community keeps the loudest voice in the room from becoming the truest. This week, find one other person and read something together that you would otherwise have read alone.\f* to see whether what they were hearing held up.
\v 12 Many of them came to believe: Greek women of standing, and men, a good number of them too.
\p
\v 13 \add It could have been a good place to stay.\add* But when the Thessalonian opponents heard that Paul was proclaiming God's word in Beroea too, they came after him there and stirred up the crowds again.
\v 14 At once the believers sent Paul off toward the sea, while Silas and Timothy stayed behind.
\v 15 The men with Paul saw him all the way to Athens. Then they started back, carrying word: Silas and Timothy were to follow as soon as they could.
\s1 Paul in Athens
\p
\v 16 Paul waited for them in Athens. Silas and Timothy hadn't caught up yet, so he had time to walk the city\add , the city that stood, like no other, for learning and philosophy\add*. And what he saw gave him no peace. The place was full of idols\add , an altar at every corner, a god in every doorway, sacred stones wedged between the houses\add*. \add The longer he looked, the less he could hold still.\add*
\p
\v 17 So he set to work. He argued in the synagogue, with Jews and with the Greeks who worshiped the God of Israel, and every day in the marketplace, with whoever happened to be there. \add Never a lecture. Always an argument.\add*
\p
\v 18 Before long the philosophers took him on: followers of Epicurus, and Stoics.\f + \fr 17:18 \fk KULTUR \fq followers of Epicurus, and Stoics \ft Two living schools of thought, not museum pieces. The Epicureans sought an unshakable calm: the world is made of atoms, the gods take no interest in us, and after death there is nothing — so live moderately, without fear. The Stoics sought virtue by aligning the self with the reason that runs through the whole cosmos. A Stoic could make something of talk about a God who is near. Neither school could do anything with the resurrection of the dead: for the one, the body is atom-dust; for the other, a prison the soul is glad to be free of.\f* Some sneered: "What is this seed-picker trying to tell us?"\add  A bird hopping the market for crumbs, a scavenger of other people's scraps.\add* Others said, "He seems to be proclaiming foreign gods." They said it because he kept talking about Jesus, and about resurrection.
\p
\v 19 So they took him up to the Areopagus\add , the council that had always decided which new gods, which foreign cults, would be given a home in Athens\add*.\f + \fr 17:19 \fk KULTUR \fq the Areopagus \ft The Areopagus was two things at once: a rocky hill below the Acropolis, and the council that met there. Once a court for cases of bloodshed, by Paul's day it watched over public order — including the question of which new gods and foreign cults the city would admit. Paul is not on a stage for free speech; he is in something closer to a hearing. There is an irony ancient listeners caught at once: the founding charter of this very court ruled a resurrection of the dead explicitly out — blood once spilled stays spilled. That is precisely where Paul will end.\f* "May we know," they asked him, "what this new teaching of yours actually is?
\v 20 What you're telling us sounds strange to our ears, and we'd like to know what it means."
\v 21 For the Athenians, and the foreigners who had settled among them, loved nothing so much as the latest thing: telling it, or hearing it.
\s1 The Speech on the Areopagus
\p
\v 22 So Paul stood up there in the middle of the council, and began.
\p
"Men of Athens, everywhere I look in this city, I see how seriously you take the gods, and how thoroughly the divine has been worked into the very texture of your common life.\f + \fr 17:22 \fk UEBERSETZUNG \fq how seriously you take the gods \ft The Greek word here can be read two ways — "superstitious," or "genuinely religious, serious about the divine." Older translations often chose the insult. But Paul is opening a speech by the rules of ancient oratory, and such an opening is built to win the audience's goodwill — it does not begin with a slap. The sense is the generous one: you take the divine with great seriousness. We translate what Paul is doing: he honors his hearers' search before he carries it further.\f*
\v 23 For as I walked through your city and looked carefully at the things you worship, I came at last upon an altar with these words cut into its stone: \nd to an unknown god\nd*.\f + \fr 17:23 \fk KULTUR \fq To an unknown god \ft Athenian devotion covered every angle. The travel-writer Pausanias describes altars "to unknown gods"; similar ones are attested elsewhere. The logic was protective: lest a god you have failed to honor turn against you, you build it an altar in advance. Paul takes up that gesture — and sharpens it, turning the many unknown gods into a single one. He does not mock the altar. He takes seriously the search inside it, and claims: what you sense without being able to name it, that I can name.\f* What you worship, then, without knowing what it is: that is what I have come to tell you.
\p
\v 24 The God who made the world and everything in it — the Lord of heaven and earth — does not live in temples built by human hands,
\v 25 nor is he tended by human hands, as though he stood in need of anything; he himself gives to every living thing its life, its breath, everything it has.
\v 26 From one\f + \fr 17:26 \fk TEXT \fq From one \ft The Greek manuscripts diverge here. The oldest read "from one"; many others read "from one blood." Both say the same decisive thing: every people descends from a single source, and no nation stands above another. The familiar English smoothing "from one man" is in no Greek manuscript at all. The verse has a bitter American history. Abolitionists read it straight — "all of one blood" — as the scriptural ground for human equality. On the other side, defenders of slavery answered with their own exegesis: Thornton Stringfellow's \em Scriptural and Statistical Views in Favor of Slavery\em* (1856), distributed across the South in the years before the war, set the template, and a long lineage of segregation-era prooftexters drew on the same maneuver — preserving the verse's words while inserting a hierarchy of peoples that the verse itself refuses. The text knows no ranking of peoples; the readers ranked them anyway.\f* he made every people of the earth, and spread them across the whole face of it; he set the times appointed for them, and the boundaries of the lands in which they live,
\v 27 all of it so that they would seek him, reach out for him\add , the way a person feels for something in the dark,\add* and perhaps in the end find him. And yet he is not far from any one of us.\f + \fr 17:27 \fk LEBEN \fq not far from any one of us \ft This is the line of the chapter for the reader who is not, by their own reckoning, "arrived." Paul is not saying that the seekers among his audience are at the doorstep, almost-but-not-yet; he is saying that what they have been reaching for — by touch, in the half-light, without certainty — has never been at any real distance. The mode of search the verse honors is tactile, not analytical: not what one has worked out, but what one feels for in the dark. If you do not know what you believe, the verse speaks about you, not against you. For those further along, the verse opens a contemplative thread that runs from the desert fathers through John of the Cross to the apophatic strand in modern theology: the God who is sought tactilely is the God whose nearness is felt before it is known. A practice the early Jesuits formalized in the examen, and Quakers in silent waiting, is built on exactly the disposition the verse names. The Berean reading habit from v. 11 deepens here into a longer arc: the same patience extended to one's own interior. And it lands beyond the personal — workplaces, friendships, civic life all run on what is reached for in the half-light long before it is articulated. This week, set aside thirty seconds at the same hour each day in which you do not try to settle the question — let it stand, and let yourself be near it. And say out loud, to one person, the thing you would normally keep to yourself.\f*
\v 28 For in him we live, in him we move, in him we are, as some of your own poets have said: We too are his children.\f + \fr 17:28 \fk KONTEXT \fq We too are his children \ft The line is a quotation — from a poem by the Greek writer Aratus, who, like Paul, came from Cilicia. The earlier phrase, "in him we live and move," also had a ring that Stoic listeners would have recognized. What is remarkable is what Paul does with it: a Jewish messenger quotes pagan poetry, not to refute it but to agree with it. Greek wisdom, at its best, has touched something true; Paul takes it up and carries it forward. It is a generosity that later Christian argument too often lost.\f*
\p
\v 29 And if indeed we are his children, then we cannot imagine that the divine in any way resembles gold, or silver, or stone, an image shaped by human craft, human imagination.
\v 30 God has let the times of not-knowing pass.\f + \fr 17:30 \fk KONTEXT \fq times of not-knowing \ft "Not-knowing" can sound dismissive — as if everything before faith were mere darkness. That is not the sense. Paul is speaking to an educated audience in the intellectual capital of the ancient world; the "not-knowing" he names is the religious confusion of that confident elite — the city of countless altars that still had to dedicate one to a god it could not name. It is not a blanket verdict on every non-Christian culture. Where the sentence was later used to write off other religions as plain darkness, that was a misuse of the text.\f* But now he calls on everyone, everywhere, to turn\add , to repent\add*.
\v 31 For he has set a day on which he will judge the world, and judge it justly, through a man he has appointed; and the proof he has given, to all, is this: he raised that man from the dead."\f + \fr 17:31 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq he raised that man from the dead \ft This is where the speech breaks — then and now. A resurrection of the dead: for a modern, skeptical reader, this is the place to get off. — The Christian tradition does not read this line as a naive boast, but as the hinge everything turns on. Paul does not argue the resurrection into being from first principles; he reports an event — something he says happened, attested by people who staked their lives on it. If it is true, it is not a pious ornament but the reason this movement exists at all: death, which had the last word for the old Athenians, does not have it here. The earliest creed Paul cites (1 Corinthians 15:3–8) lists named witnesses; the founding charter of the very court Paul is addressing had ruled a resurrection of the dead explicitly out, and that is precisely the ruling the speech reverses. That is a serious reading, not a credulous one. — If the step to a historical event is too far, the text still says something readable as image: that a justice which counts is not handed down by the powerful but by one whom the powerful killed — and that violence does not get the last word. The resurrection, on this reading, is the sentence by which the world's verdict on Jesus is overturned. — And for the reader for whom even that is too far: the comparative study of religion notes that ancient near-eastern myths of dying-and-rising deities (Osiris, Dumuzi, Adonis) circulate as seasonal cycles tied to vegetation and the agricultural year. The New Testament's claim is structurally different — a particular man, named, in a particular week, with named witnesses — but the comparative frame is where skeptical scholarship locates the passage, and one can read it that way without leaving the page. — What all the readings share: the Athenians themselves split at exactly this point. Some laughed. Some put it off. You are in good company if you hesitate. The question the text leaves open is not whether you must believe it all at once, but how far you are willing to go.\f*
\p
\v 32 At the words "raised from the dead," the meeting broke up. Some of them laughed out loud. Others said, "We'll hear you on this another time."
\v 33 And Paul left the council.
\p
\v 34 But some went with him and believed: Dionysius, a member of the council itself; a woman named Damaris;\f + \fr 17:34 \fk HARM \fq a woman named Damaris \ft Damaris is the only woman named among the converts at Athens — and one of the very few women anywhere in the New Testament to appear in a setting this learned and this public. For a long time it was assumed that a woman in such a place could only have been a courtesan. Recent scholarship — Ivoni Richter Reimer among others — has set that reading aside: more likely she was herself educated in philosophy, since the Epicurean and Pythagorean schools admitted women. One old family of manuscripts cut her name out entirely; the same tendency quietly weakens the standing of women elsewhere in Acts. Luke names her, equal to Dionysius. So do we.\f* and others besides.
